What hospitals actually use

Most hospital prediction models aren't giant language models.

Instead they're often:

Gradient-boosted decision trees (XGBoost/LightGBM)
Recurrent neural networks (LSTMs)
Temporal Convolutional Networks
Transformers designed for time-series clinical data

The important part is handling time, because patients change over hours rather than existing as a single snapshot.

For your project

Your data might look like this:

Time 0
HR = 112
BP = 97/61
Lactate = 2.4

↓

15 minutes later

HR = 118
BP = 92/58
Lactate = 2.8

↓

30 minutes later

HR = 126
BP = 86/52
Lactate = 3.5

A standard Random Forest in scikit-learn only sees one row at a time unless you manually engineer trend features.

A neural network in PyTorch can learn from the sequence directly.

