What is Med-BERT?

Med-BERT is one of the first transformer models designed specifically for electronic health records (EHRs) instead of natural language.

The key insight was:

A patient's medical history can be treated like a sentence.

Instead of words:

The
patient
developed
sepsis

it sees medical events:

Hypertension

↓

Chest pain

↓

Troponin elevated

↓

Coronary angiography

↓

PCI

↓

Discharge

Every diagnosis, medication, lab, and procedure becomes a "token."

Why BERT works

In natural language,

The dog chased the ball.

BERT learns that

dog

is related to

ball

because of surrounding context.

Med-BERT does the same thing.

Diabetes

↓

Elevated glucose

↓

Metformin

↓

HbA1c

The model learns these relationships without being explicitly programmed.

How Med-BERT is trained

The first stage is self-supervised pretraining.

Imagine hiding part of a patient's record:

Hypertension

Diabetes

[MASK]

Lisinopril

The model predicts

ACE inhibitor

or

High blood pressure

depending on how the data are tokenized.

This is similar to how language models learn missing words.

Why this matters

Instead of manually telling the model

high lactate is bad
low blood pressure is bad
rising creatinine is bad

the transformer learns those patterns directly from millions of patient records.

That is incredibly powerful.

The limitation of Med-BERT

Here's the important part.

Med-BERT mostly learns what happened.

Patient:

Diabetes

↓

Sepsis

↓

ICU

↓

Discharge

Your project is about learning how the patient is changing over time.

That is harder.

Your project is closer to a Clinical Transformer

Imagine a patient arriving in the ED.

